                <pb facs="1054" n="944" sameAs="frus:frus1931v03/pg944"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1111 Nelson, Bert N./86: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-03-28T12:00:00+08:00">March 28,
                        1931—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received March 28—5:55 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>173. [From Perkins.] Legation’s 170, March 26, 4 p.m. The Minister instructs that
                    he has sent the following telegram to the American Consul General at Hankow:
                        <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Your March 23, 4 p.m. [<hi rendition="#italic">3 p.m.?</hi>], and March
                            25, 9 p.m. [<hi rendition="#italic">3 p.m.?</hi>]. Ministry of Foreign
                            Affairs promises to have urgent telegraphic instructions issued today
                            direct to General Hsia Tou-ying at Macheng ordering him to advance
                            against Red brigands and effect release of Nelson and Tvedt. Please
                            endeavor to ascertain whether such instructions are received by General
                            Hsia and what action is taken by him.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Perkins</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
